{"name":"Redstone","hex":"#e46b71","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Redstone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname.","A census-designated place and unincorporated community in Pitkin County, Colorado."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Redstone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Redstone Arsenal","blocks":[{"text":"Redstone Arsenal is a United States Army base and census-designated place in Madison County, Alabama, adjacent to Huntsville, Alabama, in the Wheeler National Wildlife Refuge. It is part of the Huntsville-Decatur Combined Statistical Area. The arsenal hosts over 75 tenant agencies including many that are part of the Department of Defense such as the United States Space Command, NASA's largest field center, and Department of Justice agencies including the Federal Bureau of Investigation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Base_Sign.jpg/330px-Base_Sign.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redstone_Arsenal","wikidata":"Q998938"},{"title":"Redstone (rocket family)","blocks":[{"text":"The Redstone family of rockets consisted of a number of American ballistic missiles, sounding rockets and expendable launch vehicles operational during the 1950s and 1960s. The first member of the Redstone family was the PGM-11 Redstone missile, from which all subsequent variations of the Redstone were derived. The Juno 1 version of the Redstone launched Explorer 1, the first U.S. orbital satellite in 1958 and the Mercury-Redstone variation carried the first two U.S. astronauts into space in 1961. The rocket was named for the Redstone Arsenal in Huntsville, Alabama where it was developed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/The_Redstone_Family.png/330px-The_Redstone_Family.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redstone_(rocket_family)","wikidata":"Q393028"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/RSA_Munitions.jpg/500px-RSA_Munitions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/RSA_Munitions.jpg/500px-RSA_Munitions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1940s munitions production at Huntsville Arsenal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARSA_Munitions.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Medaris_vonBraun_and_Toftoy_01.jpg/500px-Medaris_vonBraun_and_Toftoy_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Medaris_vonBraun_and_Toftoy_01.jpg/500px-Medaris_vonBraun_and_Toftoy_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"RSA commander Maj. Gen. John Medaris, Wernher von Braun, and RSA deputy commander Brig. Gen. Holger Toftoy (left to right) in the 1950s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMedaris_vonBraun_and_Toftoy_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Ordnance_Missile_Command_SSI.png/500px-Ordnance_Missile_Command_SSI.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Ordnance_Missile_Command_SSI.png/500px-Ordnance_Missile_Command_SSI.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ordnance Missile Command SSI","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrdnance_Missile_Command_SSI.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Test_stand_56_01.jpg/250px-Test_stand_56_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Test_stand_56_01.jpg/500px